Let $p(x) = x^2 + ax + b$ for some real numbers $a$ and $b.$  Then
\begin{align*}
|p(x)| &= |x^2 + ax + b| \\
&= |(x + \tfrac{a}{2})^2 - \tfrac{a^2}{4} + b| \\
&= |(x + \tfrac{a}{2})^2 + (b - \tfrac{a^2}{4})|.
\end{align*}The maximum value of $|p(x)|$ on $-1 \le x \le 1$ is the maximum of $|b - \tfrac{a^2}{4}|$ and $|(1 + \tfrac{a}{2})^2 + (b - \tfrac{a^2}{4})|,$ so
\begin{align*}
\text{Munificence}(p) &= \max\{|b - \tfrac{a^2}{4}|, |(1 + \tfrac{a}{2})^2 + (b - \tfrac{a^2}{4})|\} \\
&= \max\{|b - \tfrac{a^2}{4}|, |1 + \tfrac{a}{2} + b| + \tfrac{1}{4}|.
\end{align*}We want to minimize the maximum value of the two expressions.  The minimum value of $|b - \tfrac{a^2}{4}|$ is 0, which occurs when $b = \tfrac{a^2}{4}.$  The minimum value of $|1 + \tfrac{a}{2} + b| + \tfrac{1}{4}$ is $\tfrac{1}{4},$ which occurs when $b = 0$ and $a = -2.$  Therefore, the smallest possible munificence is $\boxed{\tfrac{1}{4}}.$. The answer is \frac{1}{4}

