We can use the Shoelace Theorem to find the area of the triangle. The Shoelace Theorem states that if the vertices of a polygon are given in order, the area of the polygon is equal to $\frac{1}{2}$ times the sum of the products of the x-coordinates and y-coordinates of adjacent vertices, minus the sum of the products of the y-coordinates and x-coordinates of adjacent vertices.

Let $(4, -1)$, $(10, 3)$, and $(4, 5)$ be the vertices of the triangle. The Shoelace Theorem gives us the following expression for the area:
\begin{align*}
\text{Area} &= \frac{1}{2} \left[ (4)(3) + (10)(5) + (4)(-1) - (-1)(10) - (3)(4) - (5)(4) \right] \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left[ 12 + 50 - 4 + 10 - 12 - 20 \right] \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left[ 46 \right] \\
