[/asy] The munificence of a quadratic polynomial is the maximum value of $|p(x)|$ on the interval $-1 \le x \le 1.$  Since $p(x)$ is monic, we have $p(x) = x^2 + ax + b.$  Note that the maximum value of $|p(x)|$ on $-1 \le x \le 1$ is at most 2 times the maximum value of $|p(1)|$ and $|p(-1)|.$  Since $p(1) = 1 + a + b$ and $p(-1) = 1 - a + b,$ the maximum value of $|p(x)|$ on $-1 \le x \le 1$ is at most 2 times the maximum of $|1 + a + b|$ and $|1 - a + b|.$  We want to minimize the maximum of $|1 + a + b|$ and $|1 - a + b|.$  To do so, we can set $1 + a + b = 1 - a + b,$ which gives $a = 0.$  This means that $p(x) = x^2 + b,$ and the maximum value of $|p(x)|$ on $-1 \le x \le 1$ is at most 2 times the maximum of $|b|$ and $|1 + b|.$  To minimize the maximum of $|b|$ and $|1 + b|,$ we can set $b = -1,$ which gives a munificence of 2.  Hence, the smallest possible munificence of a monic quadratic polynomial is $\boxed{2}.$ The answer is 2

