We can use the Shoelace Theorem to find the area of the triangle. The Shoelace Theorem states that if we have a polygon with vertices $(x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), ..., (x_n, y_n)$, then the area of the polygon is given by
$$\frac{1}{2} \left| \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} (x_i y_{i+1} - y_i x_{i+1}) + (x_n y_1 - y_n x_1) \right|.$$
In this case, the vertices of the triangle are $(4, -1)$, $(10, 3)$, and $(4, 5)$, so we have
\begin{align*}
\text{Area} &= \frac{1}{2} \left| (4 \cdot 3 - (-1) \cdot 10) + (10 \cdot 5 - 3 \cdot 4) + (4 \cdot -1 - 5 \cdot 4) \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left| (12 + 10) + (50 - 12) + (-4 - 20) \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left| 22 + 38 - 24 \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \left| 36 \right| \\
&= \boxed{18}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: 18

