By the Law of Cosines,
\begin{align*}
PA^2 &= PB^2 + 2PB \cdot PC \cos \angle PBC + PC^2 \\
&= PB^2 + 2PB \cdot PC \left(1 - \frac{PB^2 + PC^2 - AB^2}{2 \cdot PB \cdot PC}\right) \\
&= PB^2 + 2PB \cdot PC - PB^2 - PC^2 + \frac{PB^2 \cdot PC^2}{PB \cdot PC} \\
&= 2PB \cdot PC - PC^2 + \frac{PB^2 \cdot PC^2}{PB \cdot PC} \\
&= 2PB \cdot PC - PC^2 + \frac{PB^2 \cdot PC^2}{PB \cdot PC}.
\end{align*}Similarly,
\[PB^2 + PC^2 = 2PA \cdot PB - AB^2 + \frac{PB^2 \cdot AB^2}{PB}.\]Adding these equations gives
\[PA^2 + PB^2 + PC^2 = 4PB \cdot PC - AB^2 - AB^2 + \frac{PB^2 \cdot PC^2}{PB} + \frac{PB^2 \cdot AB^2}{PB}.\]By the Law of Cosines again,
\[AB^2 = BC^2 + CA^2 - 2BC \cdot CA \cos \angle BAC = BC^2 + CA^2 - 2BC \cdot CA \cos 120^\circ = BC^2 + CA^2 + BC \cdot CA.\]Substituting, we get
\[PA^2 + PB^2 + PC^2 = 4PB \cdot PC - 2BC \cdot CA - 2BC \cdot CA + \frac{PB^2 \cdot PC^2}{PB} + \frac{PB^2 \cdot BC \cdot CA}{PB}.\]Adding the terms involving $PC$ and $BC$ gives
\[PA^2 + PB^2 + PC^2 = 4PB \cdot PC - 2BC \cdot CA + \frac{PB^2 \cdot PC^2}{PB} + \frac{PB^2 \cdot BC \cdot CA}{PB} + \frac{PB^2 \cdot BC \cdot CA}{PB}.\]Combining the terms involving $PB$ and $PC$ gives
\[PA^2 + PB^2 + PC^2 = 4PB \cdot PC - 2BC \cdot CA + \frac{PB^2 \cdot PC^2}{PB} + \frac{2PB^2 \cdot BC \cdot CA}{PB}.\]Finally, by the Law of Sines, $\frac{PB}{\sin \angle BPC} = \frac{PC}{\sin \angle CPA} = \frac{AB}{\sin \angle APB} = 2R,$ so
\[\frac{PB^2}{\sin^2 \angle BPC} = \frac{PC^2}{\sin^2 \angle CPA} = \frac{AB^2}{\sin^2 \angle APB} = 4R^2.\]Substituting, we get
\[PA^2 + PB^2 + PC^2 = 4PB \cdot PC - 2BC \cdot CA + \frac{PB^2 \cdot PC^2}{PB} + \frac{2PB^2 \cdot BC \cdot CA}{PB} = 4PB \cdot PC - 2BC \cdot CA + 4R^2 \cdot \frac{PC^2}{PB} + 4R^2 \cdot \frac{BC \cdot CA}{PB}.\]Thus, the constant is $\boxed{4R^2}.$. The answer is 4R^2

