### Step 2: Calculate \( q \)

The probability \( q \) is the probability that two of the slips bear a number \( a \) and the other two bear a number \( b \neq a \). There are 10 choices for \( a \) and 9 choices for \( b \) (since \( b \neq a \)). For each pair \( (a, b) \), there are \(\binom{4}{2}\) ways to choose 2 slips out of 4 for \( a \) and \(\binom{4}{2}\) ways to choose 2 slips out of 4 for \( b \).

So, the total number of favorable outcomes is \(10 \times 9 \times \binom{4}{2} \times \binom{4}{2}\).

