We have that
\[f(2^x) + xf(2^{-x}) = 1.\]Setting $x = 1,$ we get $f(2) + f(1/2) = 1.$  Setting $x = 2,$ we get $f(4) + 2f(1/4) = 1.$  Setting $x = 4,$ we get $f(16) + 4f(1/16) = 1.$  Continuing in this manner, we get
\begin{align*}
f(2^{2^n}) + 2^n f(2^{2^{n-1}}) &= 1, \\
f(2^{2^n}) + 2^n f(2^{2^{n-1}}) &= 1, \\
f(2^{2^n}) + 2^n f(2^{2^{n-1}}) &= 1, \\
&\vdots \\
f(2^{2^n}) + 2^n f(2^{2^{n-1}}) &= 1.
\end{align*}This gives us the system of equations
\begin{align*}
f(2^{2^n}) + 2^n f(2^{2^{n-1}}) &= 1, \\
f(2^{2^n}) + 2^n f(2^{2^{n-1}}) &= 1, \\
f(2^{2^n}) + 2^n f(2^{2^{n-1}}) &= 1, \\
&\vdots \\
f(2^{2^n}) + 2^n f(2^{2^{n-1}}) &= 1.
\end{align*}Since all the equations are the same, the only solution is $f(2^{2^n}) = 1$ for all $n.$  In particular, $f(2) = 1.$

