By the Law of Cosines, we have
\[PA^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc \cos A\]and similarly for the other two.  Then
\begin{align*}
PA^2 + PB^2 + PC^2 - PH^2 &= b^2 + c^2 - 2bc \cos A + b^2 + c^2 - 2bc \cos B + b^2 + c^2 - 2bc \cos C - PH^2 \\
&= 3b^2 + 3c^2 - 2bc (\cos A + \cos B + \cos C) - PH^2 \\
&= 3b^2 + 3c^2 - 2bc (\cos A + \cos B + \cos C) - (a^2 + b^2 + c^2 - 2abc \cos A) \\
&= 2b^2 + 2c^2 - 2bc (\cos A + \cos B + \cos C) - a^2 + 2abc \cos A \\
&= 2(b^2 - a^2) + 2(c^2 - b^2) + 2(c^2 - a^2) - 2bc (\cos A + \cos B + \cos C) \\
&= 2(b^2 - a^2) + 2(c^2 - b^2) + 2(c^2 - a^2) - 2bc (\cos A + \cos B + \cos C) \\
&= 2(b^2 - a^2) + 2(c^2 - b^2) + 2(c^2 - a^2) - 2bc (\cos A + \cos B + \cos C) \\
&= 2(b^2 - a^2) + 2(c^2 - b^2) + 2(c^2 - a^2) - 2bc (\cos A + \cos B + \cos C).
\end{align*}Since $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = 2R^2,$ this is a constant equal to $\boxed{2R^2}.$
The answer is: 2R^2

