Let $p(x) = x^2 + ax + b.$  Then
\begin{align*}
|p(x)| &= |x^2 + ax + b| \\
&= |(x - (-a/2))^2 - (a^2/4 - 2b)| \\
&\le (x - (-a/2))^2 + |a^2/4 - 2b|.
\end{align*}Since the maximum value of $(x - (-a/2))^2$ is $(-a/2)^2,$ the maximum value of $|p(x)|$ is
\[(-a/2)^2 + |a^2/4 - 2b|.\]Thus, the munificence of $p(x)$ is at most
\[\max_{-1 \le x \le 1} \left( (-a/2)^2 + |a^2/4 - 2b| \right).\]Note that
\[\max_{-1 \le x \le 1} \left( (-a/2)^2 + |a^2/4 - 2b| \right) = \max_{-1 \le x \le 1} \left( (-a/2)^2 + (a^2/4 - 2b) \right) = \max_{-1 \le x \le 1} \left( (-a/2)^2 + (a^2/4 - 2b) \right) = \max_{-1 \le x \le 1} \left( (-a/2)^2 + (a^2/4 - 2b) \right).\]Thus, we want
\[\max_{-1 \le x \le 1} \left( (-a/2)^2 + (a^2/4 - 2b) \right) = 21.\]This is equivalent to
\[\max_{-1 \le x \le 1} \left( a^2/4 - 2b \right) = 21.\]Thus, we want
\[\max_{-1 \le x \le 1} \left( a^2/4 - 2b \right) = 21.\]Since the maximum value of $a^2/4$ is $1,$ we want
\[1 - 2b = 21,\]so $b = -10.$  Then
\[\max_{-1 \le x \le 1} \left( a^2/4 - 2b \right) = \max_{-1 \le x \le 1} \left( a^2/4 - 2(-10) \right) = \max_{-1 \le x \le 1} \left( a^2/4 + 20 \right) = 21.\]Since $a^2/4 \le 1,$ we want $a^2/4 = 1,$ so $a = \pm 2.$  Thus, the smallest possible munificence of a monic quadratic polynomial is $\boxed{21}.$The answer is: 21

