\[ p\left(-\frac{1}{2}\right) = -\frac{69}{4} \]

Next, we find the maximum absolute value among these values:
\[ |p(-1)| = 17 \]
\[ |p(1)| = 15 \]
\[ |p\left(-\frac{1}{2}\right)| = \frac{69}{4} = 17.25 \]

The smallest possible munificence is the minimum of these absolute values:
\[ \min(17, 15, 17.25) = 15 \]

Therefore, the smallest possible munificence of a monic quadratic polynomial is \(\boxed{15}\).

