Since Kevin is hopping $\frac{1}{3}$ of the distance, he hops $\frac{1}{3}$ of the distance on the first hop, $\frac{1}{3}$ of the remaining distance on the second hop, and so on. We can calculate the total distance Kevin has hopped after five hops as follows:

\begin{align*}
\text{First hop: } \frac{1}{3} \\
\text{Second hop: } \frac{1}{3} \left(1 - \frac{1}{3}\right) = \frac{2}{9} \\
\text{Third hop: } \frac{1}{3} \left(1 - \frac{2}{9}\right) = \frac{7}{27} \\
\text{Fourth hop: } \frac{1}{3} \left(1 - \frac{7}{27}\right) = \frac{20}{81} \\
\text{Fifth hop: } \frac{1}{3} \left(1 - \frac{20}{81}\right) = \frac{61}{243}
\end{align*}

Adding these distances together, we have:

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{3} + \frac{2}{9} + \frac{7}{27} + \frac{20}{81} + \frac{61}{243} &= \frac{81}{243} + \frac{48}{243} + \frac{54}{243} + \frac{60}{243} + \frac{61}{243} \\
&= \frac{284}{243}
\end{align*}

Therefore, Kevin has hopped $\boxed{\frac{284}{243}}$ units after five hops. The answer is: \frac{284}{243}

