To find the minimum value of $|x^2 + bx + c|$ on the interval $-1 \le x \le 1$, we can use the fact that the minimum value of a quadratic function $ax^2 + bx + c$ on the interval $-1 \le x \le 1$ is given by the value of the function at the endpoints and the vertex. The vertex of the quadratic function $x^2 + bx + c$ occurs at $x = -\frac{b}{2}$. 

Let's consider the two cases: $-\frac{b}{2} \le -1$ and $-\frac{b}{2} \ge 1$. 

If $-\frac{b}{2} \le -1$, then the minimum value of $|x^2 + bx + c|$ on the interval $-1 \le x \le 1$ occurs at $x = -1$. In this case, we have:

\begin{align*}
|(-1)^2 + b(-1) + c| &= |1 - b + c| \\
&= |b - 1 + c|
\end{align*}

If $-\frac{b}{2} \ge 1$, then the minimum value of $|x^2 + bx + c|$ on the interval $-1 \le x \le 1$ occurs at $x = 1$. In this case, we have:

\begin{align*}
|(1)^2 + b(1) + c| &= |1 + b + c| \\
&= |b + 1 + c|
\end{align*}

Since we want to minimize the munificence, we want to minimize the maximum value of $|b - 1 + c|$ and $|b + 1 + c|$ on the interval $-1 \le x \le 1$. To do this, we can consider the case where $b = -2$ and $c = 2$. In this case, we have:

\begin{align*}
|b - 1 + c| &= |-2 - 1 + 2| = 1 \\
|b + 1 + c| &= |-2 + 1 + 2| = 1
\end{align*}

Therefore, the minimum value of $|x^2 + bx + c|$ on the interval $-1 \le x \le 1$ is 1. Taking the absolute value, we have:

\begin{align*}
