Note that the munificence of a quadratic polynomial is the maximum value of $|a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \dots + a_1 x + a_0|$ on the interval $-1 \le x \le 1.$  The munificence of a quadratic polynomial is equal to the distance from the maximum value of $|a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \dots + a_1 x + a_0|$ to the nearest critical point.  Therefore, we must find the minimum distance between the maximum value of $|a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \dots + a_1 x + a_0|$ and the nearest critical point.

Since $a_n$ is positive, $|a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \dots + a_1 x + a_0|$ is a convex function on $-1 \le x \le 1,$ so its maximum value is either $|a_n| + |a_{n-1}| + \dots + |a_1| + |a_0|$ or $|a_n| + |a_{n-1}| + \dots + |a_1| + |a_0| - 2.$  Therefore, the minimum distance between the maximum value of $|a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \dots + a_1 x + a_0|$ and the nearest critical point is $\frac{1}{2} \max \{|a_n|, |a_{n-1}|, \dots, |a_1|, |a_0|\}.$

Now, let $q(x) = x^2 + ax + b.$  Then the munificence of $q(x)$ is the maximum value of $|x^2 + ax + b|$ on the interval $-1 \le x \le 1,$ which is equal to $\frac{1}{2} \max \{|a|, |b|\}.$  For $q(x)$ to have minimum munificence, we must have $a = b = 0.$  Therefore, the smallest possible munificence of a monic quadratic polynomial is $\boxed{\frac{1}{2}}.$. The answer is \frac{1}{2}

